DisplayXR on Sparky Stereo OS
=============================

Which OpenXR runtime a program gets
-----------------------------------

An OpenXR program loads the runtime that the OpenXR loader finds (OpenXR
loader specification, "Linux Active Runtime Location"):

 1. XR_RUNTIME_JSON, when it is set, names the runtime manifest for that
    program alone;
 2. otherwise openxr/1/active_runtime.json (or active_runtime.<arch>.json)
    under $XDG_CONFIG_HOME (~/.config), then under each of $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
    (/etc/xdg), then under /etc: the first one found wins.

This package registers DisplayXR in the openxr1-active-runtime alternatives
group, the one Debian's Monado uses for /etc/xdg/openxr/1/active_runtime.json.
Nothing else is written: the loader specification asks a tool that changes the
active runtime to change only that link.

 - For the whole system:
     update-alternatives --config openxr1-active-runtime
   lists every installed runtime (DisplayXR's manifest is
   /etc/xdg/openxr/1/openxr_displayxr.json, Monado's
   /usr/share/openxr/1/openxr_monado.json). In automatic mode the one with the
   highest priority is active: DisplayXR 60, Monado 50. A choice made by hand
   stays until it is changed by hand (update-alternatives --auto returns to
   automatic mode).
 - For one person, whatever the system's choice is:
     mkdir -p ~/.config/openxr/1
     ln -sf /etc/xdg/openxr/1/openxr_displayxr.json ~/.config/openxr/1/active_runtime.json
   (or Monado's manifest); deleting the link returns to the system's choice.
 - For one program:
     XR_RUNTIME_JSON=/usr/share/openxr/1/openxr_monado.json program

What a person sees
------------------

DisplayXR drives 3D displays, not headsets: an OpenXR program opens a window on
the desktop. Its sim-display output, the one used when no vendor panel plug-in
is installed, is built here with two outputs only: 2D, and the two views side
by side, left first, each at the window's resolution, declared to the desktop.
Stereo KWin then shows them on every stereo display it drives; a plain 2D
monitor shows the left view. A desktop that does not take the declaration
shows the two views side by side, flat, and the runtime's log says why
("FULL_SBS: ...").

A headset is Monado's: with Monado active, a program goes to the headset, and
with no headset Monado has nothing to show it on.

The service
-----------

displayxr.socket is enabled for every user; the first program that needs the
out-of-process compositor (an XR_DXR_weave present-owner such as the DisplayXR
browser, or a workspace controller) starts displayxr.service, which ends 30
seconds after its last client. Ordinary programs run the runtime in their own
process.

